## The Ultimate Goal: A Self-Hosting System

The endgame is when your macro library can **build itself**:
```assembly
; The assembler in the assembler
macro assemble_source source*, output* {
    ; Use the same parsing tables
    ; Generate code
    ; Verify checksums
}

; The compiler in the compiler
macro compile_expression expr* {
    ; Parse, type-check, generate
}
```

This creates a feedback loop where improvements to the library improve the tooling that builds the library, enabling exponential growth in capability.

The prompt for your coding agent should now include: "Build a FASM macro library that treats every abstraction as literal machine operations with added contracts, supports incremental bootstrap verification, and enables construction of systems from embedded controllers to network servers to operating system kernels through composable, testable patterns."

This is the path to making FASM not just an assembler, but a **platform for building platforms**.